it looks like something out of a science  fiction film  a space-age paramedic jetting off to  locate  and treat a stricken hiker but this jet  pack could soon be the way  medics in remote locations and difficult  terrain  reached their patients this simulated  trial in the lake district saw the  response time cut  from 25 minutes to 90 seconds  the idea came as a result really of  being kind of enthralled by  the technology that gravity were using  and just seeing just how kind of  maneuverable it was and wondering  whether there would be an application  to use it in those kind of remote areas  the kit consists of  two mini engines on each arm and a main  pack on the back  which allows the pilot to navigate with  just their hands  richard browning was involved in this  trial and says the future is exciting  the suit we're flying at the moment in  fact there's some behind me um  that's the mark ii suit the mark iii  prototype is sitting just behind the  camera on our development table there  and that starts in 10 seconds instead of  45 seconds for these ones  and flies for more like six or seven  minutes and can carry even more weight  as  well this technology has been around for  some time  in august last year french inventor  frankie zappata succeeded in crossing  the channel  in just 22 minutes his paraffin-powered  device reach speeds of up to 110 miles  per hour  and when dealing with any casualty speed  is always a key  factor for some the jetpack paramedic  could simply deliver rapid pain relief  as they await conventional transport to  hospital  for others those vital minutes could be  the difference between life  and death ended brady sky news
 

